{#
  The guest view (FSD §8.4, Mockup 11-wand-gaeste).

  "Name + Grußzeile aus HA, gesetzt im Panel, dargestellt in großer
  Schreibschrift auf einem Hintergrundbild." [Festlegung C13]

  Everything that could be decided has been decided already, in
  ``guest_layout.py``: which face, at which size, in which colour, at which
  angle, broken into which lines. This file only draws it. That split is the
  lesson from the first recipe image — CSS re-wrapping what Python had budgeted
  for is how text runs off a canvas nobody is watching, and with the block now
  rotated it would be off the canvas twice over.

  **The text has no ground of its own** [Festlegung P23]. The lightened band of
  FSD §8.4 is gone; what carries the contrast against the dither raster of the
  photo is the **colour**, and it comes from the six Spectra primaries so the
  glyph edges stay solid instead of speckling.

  Dependency-free apart from the two file:// URLs it is given (the picture and
  the font file). Both are already on local disk when this renders.
#}<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ language }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>ePaperEngine</title>
    <style>
      @font-face {
        font-family: "epaperengine-script";
        src: url("{{ plan.font_url }}");
      }
      html,
      body {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        width: 2560px;
        height: 1440px;
        overflow: hidden;
        /* White rather than black: a guest page whose picture failed to load
           should read as a plain greeting card, not as a fault. The photo view
           uses black for the opposite reason — there, a missing picture *is*
           the failure. */
        background: #fff;
        color: #000;
      }
      .stage {
        position: relative;
        width: 2560px;
        height: 1440px;
      }
      .stage img {
        display: block;
        width: 2560px;
        height: 1440px;
        object-fit: cover;
      }
      .text {
        position: absolute;
        left: 50%;
        top: 50%;
        /* Shrink-wrapped, not full width: the box is rotated about its own
           centre, and ``guest_layout`` measured the rotated bounding box of
           exactly this box. A 2.560 px container would turn every tilt into a
           diagonal several screens wide. */
        width: max-content;
        max-width: 2240px;
        text-align: center;
        transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate({{ plan.angle }}deg);
        /* The family is named *and* declared through @font-face above; the two
           are the same file. "cursive" is the last resort and would be visible
           at a glance, which is the point of naming it rather than sans-serif. */
        font-family: "{{ plan.font_family }}", "epaperengine-script", cursive;
        font-weight: {{ plan.font_weight }};
        color: {{ plan.color }};
      }
      .line {
        white-space: nowrap;
      }
      .name .line {
        font-size: {{ plan.name.font_px }}px;
        line-height: {{ line_height }};
      }
      .greeting .line {
        font-size: {{ plan.greeting.font_px }}px;
        line-height: {{ line_height }};
      }
      /* Nothing typed yet. Not an error — the section exists from the first
         start — so it says so quietly in the one font that is always there, and
         in black, whatever colour the greeting would have had: a white notice
         on a white page would be no notice at all. */
      .empty {
        font-family: "DejaVu Sans", sans-serif;
        font-size: 56px;
        color: #000;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="stage">
      {% if plan.background_url %}<img src="{{ plan.background_url }}" alt="" />{% endif %}
      <div class="text">
        {% if plan.name.lines or plan.greeting.lines %}
        <div class="name">
          {% for line in plan.name.lines %}<div class="line">{{ line }}</div>{% endfor %}
        </div>
        {% if plan.greeting.lines %}
        {# The gap only exists between two blocks. Hard-coding it as a margin
           would add 48 px that ``guest_layout`` did not put into the block
           height when only one of the two fields is filled — and the rotated
           bounding box is measured off that height. #}
        <div class="greeting" style="margin-top: {{ block_gap if plan.name.lines else 0 }}px">
          {% for line in plan.greeting.lines %}<div class="line">{{ line }}</div>{% endfor %}
        </div>
        {% endif %}
        {% else %}
        <div class="empty">{{ t("guests.empty") }}</div>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
